raw_response:
Step-by-step analysis:

**Entity types in the schema.** Each record is a timestamped AIS position report about one vessel, so there are really two grains: (1) the vessel itself (identity and static characteristics: mmsi, imo, ship_id, shipname, callsign, flag, type_name, ais_type_summary, shiptype, market, ship_class, dimensions, year_built) and (2) the per-record observation (lat, lon, speed, heading, course, rot, status, timestamp, dsrc, utc_seconds). The ontology's extension classes bsm:Observation plus bsm:hasObservation (Vehicle → Observation) and bsm:observationDateTime exist precisely for this split, so I model one Observation per record, keyed by mmsi + timestamp.

**Vessel subject.** mmsi is the always-populated AIS identity; imo is explicitly "empty when not known" (would silently drop vessels), and ship_id is provider-internal. So the vessel IRI is vessel_{mmsi}; ship_id becomes bsm:sourceIdentifier ("primary key or row identifier from the originating dataset" — exactly MarineTraffic's internal id), and imo/mmsi become structured identifiers (below).

**AlternateIdentifier entities.** The schema carries several identifier schemes for the same hull (mmsi, imo, ship_id) — this is the explicit multi-scheme structure that justifies bsm:AlternateIdentifier (standard code + value), not a single generic "id" column. I create altid_mmsi_{mmsi} (standard "MMSI") and altid_imo_{imo} (standard "IMO"; keyed on imo so empty IMOs skip the entity cleanly). callsign instead goes to bsm:alternateName, whose comment explicitly lists "callsigns, transponder-broadcast names, vessel AIS names".

**GeopoliticalAffiliation.** bsm:hasAffiliation's comment explicitly says it "captures flag-state of vessels", and bsm:GeopoliticalAffiliation's comment says "flag state of a vessel". flag (ISO alpha-2) maps to bsm:geopoliticalAffiliationCode on a geoaff_{mmsi} entity. ship_country (full name) stays unmapped: the only name property, bsm:conceptName, has domain bsm:Concept, and bsm:Affiliation is not declared a Concept subclass, so there is no valid home for it.

**Location chain.** Observation → bsm:hasGeographicLocation → bsm:Location (loc_{mmsi}_{timestamp}) → bsm:hasGeometry → bsm:AbsolutePoint (point_{mmsi}_{timestamp}) carrying bsm:latitudeDecimal ← lat and bsm:longitudeDecimal ← lon (WGS84 decimal degrees match the AbsolutePoint comment exactly).

**Velocity vs Orientation — the near-synonym disambiguation.** bsm:absoluteBearingAngle (on Velocity) and bsm:headingAngle (on Orientation) are both "degrees from true north". The deciding qualifiers: absoluteBearingAngle's comment says "Course over ground / track angle", matching the course column ("Course over ground… direction the vessel is actually moving"); headingAngle's comment says "direction the longitudinal axis points", matching heading ("true heading of the vessel's bow"). So course → Velocity.absoluteBearingAngle, heading → Orientation.headingAngle. **speed is left unmapped**: the only speed property, bsm:speedMetersPerSecond, names meters-per-second in its local name, while the column is tenths of a knot and templates cannot convert units — the unit qualifier disagrees, so I do not force it. avg_speed and rot have no property at all.

**Vessel properties.** shipname → bsm:conceptName (primary name). type_name → bsm:typeName, whose comment explicitly covers "vessel-type strings broadcast by maritime navigation systems". For bsm:vesselCategoryCode there are three candidates; the qualifier deciding is the property's enumerated values "cargo, tanker, fishing, passenger, military": ais_type_summary's values (Tanker, Cargo, Passenger, Military) match exactly, while shiptype is a numeric ITU code and market is a commercial segment (TANKERS, NAVAL) — so ais_type_summary wins and shiptype/market stay unmapped. max_speed → bsm:vesselMaximumSpeed only at low confidence: the local names match, but the property comment says "maximum *design* speed" while the column is the maximum *recorded over the current voyage* — I flag this design-vs-observed ambiguity rather than assuming equivalence.

**Ports and voyage.** bsm:Port exists, but the ontology has no object property linking a Vessel or Observation to a Port (no destination/departure/arrival relation), so all current_port/last_port/next_port columns, plus destination, eta, eta_calc, eta_updated, last_port_time, distance_to_go and distance_travelled, have no valid mapping and are unmapped. status, dsrc, utc_seconds, dimensions (length, width, grt, dwt, draught, l_fore, w_left), year_built and ship_class likewise have no matching property.

**IRI consistency check.** Every iri-typed value's transformation was copied character-for-character from its target's subject_transformation: obs_{mmsi}_{timestamp}, loc_{mmsi}_{timestamp}, point_{mmsi}_{timestamp}, vel_{mmsi}_{timestamp}, ori_{mmsi}_{timestamp}, geoaff_{mmsi}, altid_mmsi_{mmsi}, altid_imo_{imo}. No empty transformation objects are emitted; all class/property URIs appear verbatim in the ontology.

